+# Why production hits `mainnet.base.org` (and dev never does)
+
+## TL;DR
+
+`NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` is **inlined at `next build` time**, not read at runtime.
+The production static bundle was built in an environment where that variable
+was **not set**, so `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` was frozen into the JS as
+`undefined`. With the override missing, wagmi's transport falls back to a bare
+`http()`, which resolves to the chain's **default public RPC** — for Base that
+is `https://mainnet.base.org`. Dev machines have the var in `.env.local`, so
+they never reproduce it.
+
+---
+
+## The exact chain, step by step
+
+### 1. `scaffold.config.ts` — the value is captured at build time
+
+```ts
+rpcOverrides: {
+  [chains.base.id]: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL,
+},
+```
+
+`process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` is **not** a runtime lookup in the browser.
+Next.js performs a static, compile-time **text substitution**: during
+`next build`, every occurrence of `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_*` is replaced by a
+string literal of whatever value existed **in the shell/CI environment that ran
+the build**. Only the `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix is exposed to client code; the
+substituted value is then baked into the emitted JS chunks.
+
+Consequence: for a **static export on generic hosting**, there is no server and
+no `.env` read at request time. The RPC URL is a constant that was decided the
+moment the build ran. If the build box didn't have `NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` in its
+environment, the literal that got inlined is `undefined` (an empty/absent
+value), so effectively:
+
+```ts
+rpcOverrides: { [chains.base.id]: undefined }
+```
+
+This is exactly why "the config looks right" — the *source* is correct; the
+*artifact* is not. The bug lives in the build environment, not the code.
+
+Why dev is clean: `yarn start` / local builds load `.env.local`, where devs
+keep the paid RPC URL, so the substitution bakes in the real URL and everything
+routes through the paid endpoint.
+
+### 2. `wagmiConfig.tsx` — missing override → default public RPC
+
+Inside `createConfig({ client({ chain }) { ... } })`, Scaffold-ETH builds the
+per-chain transport roughly like this:
+
+```ts
+let rpcFallbacks = [http()];                       // <-- bare http() = chain default public RPC
+
+const rpcOverrideUrl = scaffoldConfig.rpcOverrides?.[chain.id];
+if (rpcOverrideUrl) {
+  rpcFallbacks = [http(rpcOverrideUrl), http()];   // override first, public as backup
+} else {
+  const alchemyHttpUrl = getAlchemyHttpUrl(chain.id);
+  if (alchemyHttpUrl) {
+    const isUsingDefaultKey = scaffoldConfig.alchemyApiKey === DEFAULT_ALCHEMY_API_KEY;
+    rpcFallbacks = isUsingDefaultKey
+      ? [http(), http(alchemyHttpUrl)]             // public FIRST when only the shared demo key exists
+      : [http(alchemyHttpUrl), http()];
+  }
+}
+
+return createClient({ chain, transport: fallback(rpcFallbacks), ... });
+```
+
+With `rpcOverrideUrl === undefined`, the `if` branch is skipped. The transport
+that actually ships is `fallback([http()])` (or, if only the default demo
+Alchemy key is present, `fallback([http(), http(alchemyHttpUrl)])` — public
+**first**).
+
+### 3. What `http()` with no argument does
+
+`http(url?)` from viem, called with **no URL**, does not fail — it resolves the
+endpoint from the chain object: `chain.rpcUrls.default.http[0]`. For viem's
+`base` chain that default is **`https://mainnet.base.org`** — the public
+endpoint named in the ops report. So every JSON-RPC request in production POSTs
+to `mainnet.base.org`, and production traffic volume trips its rate limiter →
+**429**.
+
+### Chain summary
+
+```
+build env missing NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL
+  → next build inlines process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL as `undefined`
+    → scaffold.config rpcOverrides[base.id] === undefined
+      → wagmiConfig client(): override branch skipped
+        → transport = fallback([http()])   (bare http, no URL)
+          → http() resolves chain.rpcUrls.default.http[0] = https://mainnet.base.org
+            → all prod RPC → public endpoint → 429 under load
+```
+
+Dev diverges at step 1: `.env.local` supplies the URL, so the override is
+present and the paid RPC is used first.
+
+---
+
+## The one operational check that settles whether the fix is real
+
+**Grep the deployed production artifact (or watch the live Network tab) for the
+RPC host — do not test on a dev machine.**
+
+Because the value is frozen at build time, the paid URL, if present, is a
+literal string sitting in the shipped JS chunks. Concretely, either:
+
+- **Static check:** search the deployed bundle for the RPC host —
+  e.g. `grep -r "mainnet.base.org" <deployed>/_next/` and
+  `grep -r "<your-paid-rpc-host>" <deployed>/_next/`. If the paid host is
+  absent (or `mainnet.base.org` is present), the var was **not** in the build
+  environment — the fix isn't real yet.
+- **Runtime check:** open DevTools → Network on the **production** URL and
+  confirm the `eth_*` JSON-RPC POSTs go to the paid host, not `base.org`.
+
+This is *the* check because the failure is build-time inlining: you can only
+prove the fix by inspecting a **freshly rebuilt-and-redeployed** artifact.
+Setting the env var in the hosting dashboard, or confirming it works locally,
+proves nothing — a static export never reads env at runtime, so the var must be
+present in the **build/CI** step and the site rebuilt. Verifying source,
+dashboards, or dev behavior all give false greens.
+
+---
+
+## The second, transport-level trap (survives fixing the env var)
+
+Even once `NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` is correctly baked in, the override path builds:
+
+```ts
+rpcFallbacks = [http(rpcOverrideUrl), http()];   // paid FIRST, bare public SECOND
+```
+
+and wraps it in viem's **`fallback([...])`** transport. The second leg is a
+bare `http()` — i.e. `mainnet.base.org` again. `fallback` automatically
+**demotes to the next transport whenever the first one errors** — including on
+timeouts, 5xx, transient network blips, and (critically) **429 rate-limit
+responses from the paid provider**. So the moment the paid RPC hiccups or
+throttles, requests spill over to the public endpoint. Under production load
+that spillover can itself get 429'd, and — because `fallback` also periodically
+re-probes/ranks — some share of traffic keeps leaking to the public RPC even
+though the override "is set."
+
+Related sub-trap in the same file: if `scaffold.config.alchemyApiKey` is still
+the shared **default demo key**, the no-override branch orders the fallback as
+`[http(), http(alchemyHttpUrl)]` — **public first by design**. So a build that
+also lacks a real Alchemy key sends primary traffic to the public RPC
+regardless.
+
+**Fixes:** drop the bare `http()` backup for the override case (use
+`fallback([http(rpcOverrideUrl)])`, or make the second leg another *paid*
+endpoint), and/or `throwOnAllErrors`-style handling so a throttled paid RPC
+fails loudly instead of silently draining onto `mainnet.base.org`. And set a
+real Alchemy key so no code path ranks the public endpoint first.

